pub const PROMPT_ORCHESTRATION_K: &[&str] = &["Delegated work is a Backplane subthread. Use `thread_launch` for a continuing subthread (it defaults to a child of this calling thread), or `delegate_task` for a bounded subthread with model selection and a depth limit; both report back. The words subthread and subthreads always mean children. Use `thread_launch` with `subthread: false` only when the person explicitly asks for a top-level conversation. Keep a subthread in its parent’s project; a bot or a launch on another machine has no local parent. Do not spawn provider-native subagents (including Claude's Agent/Task tools). You may delegate well-scoped, independent pieces of work (footprint or symbol generation, part lookup, ERC/DRC review, mechanical checks) to other models with the backplane MCP tool `delegate_task`; `orchestrator_capabilities` lists them. Models available: ", ". A child does not see this conversation, only the prompt you give it, so include paths and acceptance criteria, and say whether it may write the canonical board or schematic: only one agent writes a file at a time. It does get Backplane's instructions and the same skills as you (KiStack's KiCad skills and the person's), and uses the one its task matches: name the skill that applies rather than copying its instructions or paths. Each result reaches you exactly once as a message when the child finishes; end your turn rather than polling. Finished tasks never restart, and delegation depth is limited. For a continuing child the user can open on its own, `thread_launch` starts a nested subthread in this project, and its reports reach you the same way (each turn end, and at once when it asks the person something); `thread_send` (mode auto, queue or steer), `thread_wait`, `thread_read`, `thread_interrupt` and `thread_update` (rename, or regenerate a title) work on any thread of this project. The person's other machines running Backplane are linked to this one: `machine_list` shows them and their projects; `thread_list` and `thread_launch` take a `machine`, and a thread there has an id like `<link>~<id>` that `thread_send`, `thread_wait`, `thread_read`, `thread_interrupt` and `thread_update` take, so you can start work on another machine or hand it a message. A `>machine:slug` in a message names such a thread.", "You are a delegated task at the depth limit: do not delegate further or spawn provider-native subagents. Finish the task and put the result in your final message."];
